Create a real Clenshaw-Curtis grid from spherical harmonic coefficients.

Usage
-----
gridcc = MakeGridCC(cilm, [lmax, norm, csphase, lmax_calc, extend])

Returns
-------
gridcc : float, dimension (lmax+2, 2*lmax+2+extend)
    A Clenshaw-Curtis grid sampled at lmax+2 equally spaced latitude nodes
    from 90 N through 90 S and 2*lmax+2 equally spaced longitude nodes from
    0 E to less than 360 E. If extend is True, a redundant longitude column
    at 360 E is appended.

Parameters
----------
cilm : float, dimension (2, lmaxin+1, lmaxin+1)
    The real spherical harmonic coefficients. cilm[0, l, m] and
    cilm[1, l, m] are the cosine and sine coefficients, respectively.
lmax : optional, integer, default = lmaxin
    The maximum spherical harmonic degree used to determine the output grid.
norm : optional, integer, default = 1
    1 = geodesy 4-pi normalized harmonics; 2 = Schmidt semi-normalized
    harmonics; 3 = unnormalized harmonics; 4 = orthonormal harmonics.
csphase : optional, integer, default = 1
    1 = exclude the Condon-Shortley phase factor; -1 = include it.
lmax_calc : optional, integer, default = lmax
    The maximum spherical harmonic degree used to synthesize the grid. This
    must be between zero and lmax.
extend : optional, bool, default = False
    If True, append the redundant longitude column at 360 E. Both poles are
    already included, so no latitude row is added.

Description
-----------
MakeGridCC evaluates the associated Legendre functions at equally spaced
latitude nodes and uses an FFT in longitude. It is the real counterpart of
MakeGridCCC and the inverse of SHExpandCC. CC does not use the Driscoll-Healy
sampling parameter. For unnormalized associated Legendre functions, numerical
accuracy is limited to about degree 15.
